  <section>
    <h2>Stratton Dispensary Overview</h2>
    <p>Stratton is a small western Maine mountain town at the intersection of Route 16 and the trailheads for the Bigelow Range and Sugarloaf. The town's adult-use retail market is a single storefront (Northland Botanicals, a Cannabis Cured location) on Main Street. The Cannabis Cured brand is one of the larger Maine-grown retail chains, and the Stratton location serves both local residents and the steady stream of outdoor recreation visitors passing through to Sugarloaf, the Bigelow Range, and the surrounding high peaks.</p>
    <p>For skiers, hikers, and anglers heading into the western Maine mountains, the in-town storefront is a convenient stop on Route 16 before reaching the trailheads and ski areas.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Dispensaries in Stratton</h2>
    <p>The table below lists the licensed adult-use storefront currently operating in Stratton. Hours, menus, and pricing change frequently — always verify directly with the operator before making the drive.</p>
    <table aria-label="Licensed dispensaries in Stratton, Maine">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Operator</th><th scope="col">Address</th><th scope="col">Website</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Northland Botanicals, LLC (Cannabis Cured)</td><td>152 Main Street</td><td>https://cannabiscured.com/stratton-rec</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>All storefronts are licensed by the Maine Office of Cannabis Policy (OCP) for both adult-use and medical sales. Medical patients pay only the 5.5% state sales tax; adult-use customers pay the 10% state excise plus 5.5% sales tax on top of sticker price.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to Expect on a First Visit</h2>
    <p>If you are 21 or older with a valid government-issued photo ID (Maine driver's license, out-of-state ID, or passport), you can walk into any adult-use dispensary in Stratton and purchase. The process is the same as buying alcohol — ID check at the door, browse the menu, transact at the counter, and walk out with a sealed exit bag.</p>
    <p>First-time visitors should expect a brief intake — the <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-staffing-licensing">budtender</a> will ask if you have used cannabis before, what effects you are looking for, and whether you have any preferences on format (flower, edible, vape, concentrate). Stratton's operator is used to serving a mix of local residents and outdoor recreation visitors.</p>
    <p>For medical patients, bring your Maine medical cannabis card (or a valid out-of-state card if your state has a reciprocity agreement with Maine). Medical patients have access to higher-dose products and pay only the 5.5% state sales tax — a meaningful discount on larger purchases.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Pricing in Stratton</h2>
    <p>Pricing at Stratton dispensaries is competitive for a remote western Maine mountain market, with the usual seasonal and competitive variation. As of mid-2026:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Flower (eighth, 3.5g):</strong> $30-50, premium indoor can run higher</li>
      <li><strong>Pre-rolls (single 1g):</strong> $12-18, multi-packs $25-50</li>
      <li><strong>Edibles (100mg THC pack):</strong> $20-35, depending on format and brand</li>
      <li><strong>Distillate cartridges (1g):</strong> $35-50</li>
      <li><strong>Concentrates (1g):</strong> $40-80, depending on quality and format</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Medical patients save the 10% state excise — a meaningful discount on larger purchases. Adult-use customers should ask about first-time buyer promotions, birthday discounts, and weekday specials, which are common in this market.</p>
  </section>

